config MM_KASAN_REGIONS
	int "Number of KASAN regions"
	default 8
	---help---
		Maximum number of memory regions that can be registered with KASAN.
		Registration of a region beyond this limit is refused with a
		message and that region is left unchecked.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_AT_BOOT
	bool "Disable KASAN at boot time"
	default y
	---help---
		When enabled, KASAN checking is disabled at boot time. This allows
		the system to boot without KASAN detecting issues during initialization.

		KASAN can be enabled later through a kasan command when you want to
		start checking for memory errors. This is useful for:
		- Avoiding false positives during boot
		- Enabling KASAN only when running specific tests
		- Production builds where boot-time checking is not needed

		If disabled (n), KASAN starts checking immediately after the kernel
		heap is registered during boot.

		For now, keep this enabled (y) and use the kasan command to enable
		checking when needed.

config MM_KASAN_SELFTEST
	bool "Self test"
	default n
	---help---
		Build kernel/debug/kasan_selftest.c, which performs one deliberate
		memory error against the kernel heap on request, and the
		kasan_test application which drives it.

		A system with no memory errors and a system where instrumentation
		was never emitted are both silent, so a clean boot on its own says
		nothing about whether KASan is working. This is what tells them
		apart. Enable it for bring up and for a CI job, and turn it off
		once the port is trusted on a board.

		Every case but the in bounds one is expected to report and to
		assert. That is the pass condition. Do not enable this on a
		product image.

		Requires BUILTIN_APPS so the application registers as a TASH
		command.

config MM_KASAN_START_MANUAL
	bool "Leave checking off until it is asked for"
	default n
	---help---
		Register the regions and maintain the shadow map as usual, but do
		not begin reporting until something calls kasan_start().

		Without this, checking goes live the moment the first heap
		registers, which is very early in os_start(). The whole of boot
		then runs instrumented: drivers, Wi-Fi, the filesystem and the
		binary manager. That is what you want eventually, but it is not
		where to begin, because a report from any of them stops the board
		before it reaches a prompt.

		With this set the board boots silently and you arm KASan when you
		are ready, so a report can only come from the code you are
		testing.

		The shadow map is still maintained while disarmed. Every
		allocation and free keeps it accurate, so when you do arm, blocks
		handed out during boot are tracked correctly and a later use
		after free of one of them is still caught.

		What is lost is anything that went wrong while disarmed: KASan
		reports at the moment of access, never retroactively. Once your
		own tests are clean, boot once with this off, because the boot
		path is where driver and vendor bugs surface.

		The kasan_test application arms and disarms it from the shell.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_NULL_POINTER_CHECK
	bool "Do not check for NULL pointer access"
	default n
	---help---
		When enabled, KASAN will not check for NULL pointer accesses.

config MM_KASAN
	bool "KASAN support"
	default y
	---help---
		Enable Kernel Address Sanitizer (KASAN) support for detecting
		memory errors such as out-of-bounds accesses and use-after-free.

config MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
	bool "Instrument code for KASAN"
	default y
	depends on MM_KASAN
	---help---
		Enable instrumentation of code for KASAN. This adds checks to
		memory accesses to detect errors.

config MM_KASAN_GENERIC
	bool "Generic KASAN mode"
	default y
	depends on MM_KASAN
	---help---
		Enable generic KASAN mode which uses a shadow memory region to
		track memory accesses.

config MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT_ALL
	bool "Instrument all code for KASAN"
	default y
	depends on MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
	---help---
		Instrument all code including kernel internals. Without this,
		only application code is instrumented.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_READS_CHECK
	bool "Disable checking of read accesses"
	default y
	depends on MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
	---help---
		When enabled, KASAN will only check write accesses, not reads.
		This reduces overhead but may miss some memory errors.